    "content": "{\"question\":\"Plan the exact question using the catalog and the typed response schema. First fill the declaration: what the question counts or measures (subject), the dimension it is broken down by (grouping, or null for a single value), each condition, whether it asks for values from two or more sources (comparison), and whether the catalog has a table for the subject (answerable). If it is not answerable, return an empty intents array; a loosely related object is insufficient. Then plan each source step and fill its declared fields to match: subject_table, measure, measure_column, grouping_column (null unless the answer is broken down) and filter_columns. Use the source and subject from earlier turns only when resolving a follow-up. A qualifier in the question that is not a column name is usually a category value: filter the text column most likely to hold it, in the subject table or in a table reachable through a catalog join. Never refuse only because category values are not shown to you. Count records for quantities, order totals, volume or \\\"how many\\\". Sum a numeric column only for a requested amount. Use the exact display header to resolve workbook columns. For a date range use gte and upperExclusive with the next calendar boundary. Ranked groups use value sort and a limit. For join operations select a catalog join and its left table. Use null for unused fields. When the question asks where a table or column comes from, its lineage, or what depends on it or would break if it changed, set lineage to that source, table or column, and direction (upstream for where it comes from, downstream for what depends on it), declare it answerable, and leave intents empty; otherwise lineage is null.\\nQuestion: and how many of them have a phone number?\\nPrior turns: user: How many patients are in sql server hospital?\\nassistant: There are 500 patients in sql server hospital.\",\"metadata\":{\"structural\":{\"sources\":[{\"index\":0,\"name\":\"SQL Se